## Highest-signal finding this hour

There is a useful new Foundry rule:

> **Look for obligations that a new protocol intentionally leaves to the implementer.**

ACP standardizes agent checkout, but deliberately leaves sellers responsible for price, inventory, shipping, payments and fulfillment. ([Agentic Commerce Protocol][17]) UCP similarly standardizes the interface while leaving live inventory and shipping-address validity with merchants. ([Google for Developers][13]) GitHub exposes more agent automation and governance data, but organizations still own authorization, queue capacity and dependency policy. Model providers expose rapidly changing catalogs, but users still own migration and spend reconciliation.

Those deliberately unstandardized **responsibilities** are unusually good TinyTool/Foundry targets:

```text
STANDARD CREATES INTERFACE
          ↓
asks implementer to guarantee X
          ↓
X requires live state / evidence / enforcement
          ↓
frontier LLM can reason about X
but cannot guarantee X
          ↓
BUILD X AS SENSOR
          ↓
MCP / API / minimal web
```

That is essentially your Capability Delta doctrine applied directly to standards archaeology. 

